WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
The longest questions, worth six to nine marks,

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.760
can decide a band.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.360
In six years of NESA's marking feedback for

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.880
Chemistry and Biology, every report flagged answers that

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.760
ignored the stimulus or copied it without explaining

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.640
it, and more than half flagged judgements without

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.600
reasons.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.760
So here's how to plan a long answer

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.160
in a minute, build it one idea per

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.360
paragraph, use the stimulus, and finish with a

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.200
judgement when the verb asks.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.440
Long answers usually aren't marked by ticking separate

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:32.080
points.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.120
A marker reads the whole answer and places

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.080
it in a band.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.040
In recent guidelines, the top band for this

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.520
kind of question asks for detailed use of

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.560
the stimulus and a well-supported judgement.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.040
So the target isn't more words.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.400
It's every demand covered, with evidence, in an

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.160
order a marker can follow.

00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:56.006
Here's an HSC-style Biology question.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:00.246
Region R's measles vaccination coverage fell after some

00:01:00.327 --> 00:01:03.446
clinics closed, and a school catch-up program began

00:01:03.527 --> 00:01:04.886
in twenty eighteen.

00:01:05.207 --> 00:01:07.766
The table gives coverage and cases from twenty

00:01:07.927 --> 00:01:10.086
ten to twenty twenty two.

00:01:10.207 --> 00:01:11.126
Seven marks.

00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:13.206
Read it, and plan what you'd write.

00:01:13.607 --> 00:01:15.206
Take a full minute if you like.

00:01:15.447 --> 00:01:16.726
Pause here to answer.

00:01:27.134 --> 00:01:29.893
Step one: take the question apart.

00:01:30.174 --> 00:01:31.533
There are two verbs.

00:01:31.854 --> 00:01:34.093
Analyse the data to explain the change in

00:01:34.174 --> 00:01:37.853
cases, and assess the catch-up program.

00:01:37.894 --> 00:01:41.533
There's a constraint: refer to immunity in individuals

00:01:41.694 --> 00:01:43.373
and in the population.

00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:45.693
And there's the stimulus, which you're expected to

00:01:45.774 --> 00:01:46.653
use.

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:49.453
That's four demands, so write four small ticks

00:01:49.534 --> 00:01:51.533
in the margin, and tick each one as

00:01:51.614 --> 00:01:52.253
you cover it.

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:57.066
Step two: assess asks for a judgement, so

00:01:57.227 --> 00:01:58.426
decide it first.

00:01:58.907 --> 00:02:02.106
Here, the program probably contributed, with a limit.

00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:04.826
Then jot one line per paragraph.

00:02:05.307 --> 00:02:07.066
Vaccine, memory cells.

00:02:07.547 --> 00:02:09.786
Ninety five per cent, herd immunity.

00:02:10.347 --> 00:02:12.506
Coverage fell, cases rose.

00:02:12.987 --> 00:02:15.626
Catch-up, cases fell, plus a limit.

00:02:16.347 --> 00:02:18.186
Next to each line, write the number from

00:02:18.227 --> 00:02:19.386
the table it'll use.

00:02:19.787 --> 00:02:21.439
That's your plan, and it takes about a

00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:21.946
minute.

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:25.726
Now build each paragraph the same way.

00:02:25.967 --> 00:02:26.926
Make the point.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:29.806
Give the evidence from the stimulus, with numbers.

00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:32.206
Explain the biology that links them.

00:02:32.607 --> 00:02:34.366
Then link back to the question.

00:02:35.247 --> 00:02:36.366
Take paragraph three.

00:02:37.007 --> 00:02:39.486
Coverage fell from ninety five to eighty seven

00:02:39.647 --> 00:02:40.686
per cent.

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:43.646
With fewer people immune, an infected person met

00:02:43.727 --> 00:02:47.006
more susceptible people, so chains of transmission kept

00:02:47.087 --> 00:02:49.886
going, and cases rose from eight to three

00:02:49.967 --> 00:02:51.326
hundred and ten.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:54.046
So the fall in coverage explains the outbreak.

00:02:55.267 --> 00:02:57.586
The first two paragraphs cover the constraint.

00:02:58.067 --> 00:03:01.266
One for the individual: the vaccine presents measles

00:03:01.387 --> 00:03:04.146
antigens without the disease, so the body makes

00:03:04.227 --> 00:03:06.706
memory cells, and a later infection meets a

00:03:06.787 --> 00:03:09.026
fast secondary response.

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:11.746
One for the population: when coverage is near

00:03:11.827 --> 00:03:14.306
the ninety five per cent target, most people

00:03:14.347 --> 00:03:16.813
are immune, so the virus rarely finds a

00:03:16.867 --> 00:03:19.186
new host, which fits the low cases of

00:03:19.307 --> 00:03:21.426
twenty ten and twenty twelve.

00:03:21.507 --> 00:03:24.306
One idea per paragraph keeps each link easy

00:03:24.387 --> 00:03:24.946
to see.

00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:29.246
Using the stimulus means more than copying it.

00:03:29.527 --> 00:03:32.166
A weak answer says cases went up, then

00:03:32.247 --> 00:03:32.646
down.

00:03:33.127 --> 00:03:35.686
A strong one picks the values that matter,

00:03:35.807 --> 00:03:38.966
compares them, and says what they mean: coverage

00:03:39.047 --> 00:03:41.686
dropped below the ninety five per cent target,

00:03:41.807 --> 00:03:43.526
and that's when cases climbed.

00:03:44.087 --> 00:03:46.406
Quote the number, then interpret it.

00:03:47.634 --> 00:03:50.593
Assess asks for a judgement, so state it

00:03:50.674 --> 00:03:52.593
in your first line, and come back to

00:03:52.674 --> 00:03:53.393
it at the end.

00:03:54.194 --> 00:03:56.833
Before the program, from twenty sixteen to twenty

00:03:56.914 --> 00:04:00.033
eighteen, cases had already fallen from three hundred

00:04:00.114 --> 00:04:02.433
and ten to a hundred and eighty.

00:04:02.594 --> 00:04:05.233
After it began, coverage rose from eighty nine

00:04:05.394 --> 00:04:07.953
to ninety five per cent, and cases fell

00:04:08.074 --> 00:04:09.313
to six.

00:04:09.394 --> 00:04:12.033
These data can't separate the program's effect from

00:04:12.154 --> 00:04:13.313
other changes.

00:04:13.354 --> 00:04:16.113
So the fair judgement is: the program probably

00:04:16.274 --> 00:04:19.153
contributed, though we can't say how much.

00:04:19.234 --> 00:04:21.633
A judgement with its condition is stronger than

00:04:21.714 --> 00:04:22.833
a bare yes.

00:04:24.034 --> 00:04:26.193
Put two answers side by side.

00:04:26.434 --> 00:04:29.713
The weak one describes the trend, explains vaccination

00:04:29.794 --> 00:04:32.513
in general, and ends with, so the program

00:04:32.594 --> 00:04:35.033
was good because vaccines are important.

00:04:35.314 --> 00:04:38.273
It covers one verb, uses no numbers, and

00:04:38.354 --> 00:04:40.433
its judgement has no reasons.

00:04:40.754 --> 00:04:43.313
The strong one covers both verbs and both

00:04:43.394 --> 00:04:46.593
kinds of immunity, uses the table's values, and

00:04:46.754 --> 00:04:48.793
judges the program with a condition.

00:04:49.074 --> 00:04:50.993
Same science, different band.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.000
Your turn.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.560
Do the one in your subject, or both.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.840
Biology first.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.520
A ward started hand hygiene training in July,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.360
and in the same month a new cleaning

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:03.000
routine.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.200
Compliance and infection rates are on screen.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.800
The question says evaluate the training program, for

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:10.600
six marks.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.200
Don't write the answer.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.120
Write the plan: the demands, your judgement, and

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:17.720
one line per paragraph.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:19.480
Pause here to answer.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:33.900
Judgement first: probably effective, but not proven.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.820
Line one: hand hygiene breaks the contact link.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.740
Line two: compliance rose from forty five to

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.460
eighty two per cent while infections fell from

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.620
six to two point five.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.660
Line three: the cleaning routine started the same

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.860
month, so training can't take all the credit.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.940
The habit to drop: starting to write without

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:56.700
a plan.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.020
Now Chemistry.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.460
The question: evaluate the use of soap for

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:05.180
washing clothes in a town with hard water.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:08.460
A student explains the scum, the wasted soap

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.940
and the grey deposits, and that soap is

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.300
renewable and breaks down readily.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:17.260
Her last line is, overall, soap has advantages

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:18.700
and disadvantages.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.780
Rewrite that line so it makes a judgement.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:23.340
Pause here to answer.

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:36.246
A judgement takes a side, and gives the

00:06:36.327 --> 00:06:37.046
reason.

00:06:37.287 --> 00:06:37.926
For example:

00:06:38.487 --> 00:06:41.126
in this hard-water town, soap is a poor

00:06:41.207 --> 00:06:44.246
choice for washing clothes, because calcium ions turn

00:06:44.327 --> 00:06:46.806
much of it into scum, which wastes soap

00:06:47.047 --> 00:06:49.446
and marks the fabric, even though soap is

00:06:49.527 --> 00:06:50.806
renewable.

00:06:50.967 --> 00:06:54.486
Advantages and disadvantages, with no decision, is the

00:06:54.567 --> 00:06:55.366
trap.

00:06:55.447 --> 00:06:57.526
It's a summary, not a judgement.

00:06:58.734 --> 00:07:01.453
So, for a long answer: take the question

00:07:01.534 --> 00:07:03.613
apart, and tick each demand.

00:07:04.094 --> 00:07:06.493
If the verb asks for a judgement, decide

00:07:06.574 --> 00:07:07.293
it first.

00:07:07.854 --> 00:07:10.493
Plan one line per paragraph, with the evidence

00:07:10.534 --> 00:07:11.293
it'll use.

00:07:11.774 --> 00:07:15.853
Build each paragraph as point, evidence, explain, link.

00:07:16.334 --> 00:07:18.253
And when the verb asks, end with the

00:07:18.334 --> 00:07:20.333
judgement and its condition.

00:07:20.814 --> 00:07:25.453
Next in Biology: practise evaluating disease control strategies.

00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:28.106
Here are the key notes.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:30.266
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:30.347 --> 00:07:31.146
to copy them.
